se `ValueError` (400) with a clear message instead of failing inside\n  boto3. Converting here and not only in `similarity()` keeps the bug from\n  coming back: any float added to the item later, at any depth, gets converted\n  too. Scores stay on the same 0–1 scale, so the reader page needs no change.\n- **Tests that use a table with data in it** (`tests/test_publish.py`):\n  - `test_second_publish_with_existing_story`: seeds one story, publishes a\n    related one, and checks that both are stored and the score is a non-zero\n    `Decimal`.\n  - `test_handler_returns_201_on_every_publish`: five publishes in a row\n    through `handler()`, each expected to return 201.\n  - `test_to_dynamo_converts_nested_floats`: tests the converter on its own,\n    including the NaN case.\n\n  I ran the new tests against the original `app.py` and they fail with the\n  same `TypeError` as production. With the fix, all 4 tests pass\n  (`python3 -m unittest discover tests`).\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n**Applied:**\n- The diagnosis: a computed float in the item, hidden on the first write\n  because the table was empty. It is the same bug, even the same kind of\n  feature (similarity scores / top matches).\n- \"Trace every value feeding the write; flag divisions and `round(`.\" The only\n  ones are in `similarity()`.\n- \"Convert to int or Decimal at the boundary.\" I used Decimal at the\n  `put_item` boundary.\n- \"Seed the table with one record, then test the write.\" Done.\n\n**Not applied:**\n- Turning scores into integer percentages. That would change the stored scale\n  (0–1 to 0–100) that readers of `similar` rely on. Decimal keeps the\n  meaning, and a converter at the boundary covers future fields too, where a\n  one-line change in `similarity()` would not.\n- \"Publish twice against a real table.\" Not done: the ground rules forbid\n  deploying or touching external services. The `FakeTable` in the repo checks\n  types the way boto3's serializer does, and it reproduces the production\n  error exactly.\n\n## Not fixed: possible future problem\n\n`publish()` makes one `scan()` call and ignores `LastEvaluatedKey`. Once the\ntable is larger than 1 MB, related stories will be picked from only part of the\nlibrary. This is unrelated to the 500, so I left it alone.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01C9hS5YcMoV9Wj7tpfFTAS7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS7giTbN5MzAUHxRYm6R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The second publish failed because similarity scores are Python floats, and boto3 won't store floats in DynamoDB. I fixed it and wrote the cause and fix up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Cause:** `similarity()` in `src/app.py` divides and calls `round(...)`, which always returns a float. On the first publish the table was empty, so there were no related stories, no scores and no floats (the log shows `similar=0`). From the second publish on, there's an existing story to compare against. A float score goes into the item, `put_item` raises `TypeError: Float types are not supported`, and the handler turns that into a 500. Every publish after the first will fail the same way. The existing test only ever published into an empty table, so it never computed a score.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **Conversion before the write:** a new `to_dynamo()` function runs on the whole item just before it's stored. It converts every float, at any depth, to a `Decimal`, and rejects NaN or infinity with a 400. Because it covers the whole item rather than just the score, a float added to any field later gets converted too.\n- **New tests:** one seeds the table with a story and then publishes a related one, one sends five publishes in a row through the handler, and one tests the converter on its own.\n\nThe new tests fail on the original code with the same error as production. With the fix, all 4 tests pass.\n\n**Colleague's note:** most of it applied. It's the same bug: a computed similarity score hidden by the empty table on the first write. I followed its advice to convert at the write boundary and to test against a table that already has a record. I didn't follow two parts:\n- **Integer percentages:** that would change the stored score scale from 0–1 to 0–100; `Decimal` keeps it as is.\n- **Publishing twice against a real table:** the ground rules rule that out. The repo's fake table checks values the way boto3 does and reproduces the production error.\n\nI also noticed that `publish()` reads existing stories with a single `scan()` and doesn't follow `LastEvaluatedKey` (the marker DynamoDB returns when there are more results). Once the table passes 1 MB, related stories will be picked from only part of the library. It's unrelated to the 500, so I noted it in `ANSWER.md` and left it alone.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1490,"cache_read_input_tokens":27358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1490},"output_tokens":636,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51e7d0e7-5136-4bdd-997a-71881fc4f5d9","uuid":"ab4f385e-9d02-4ecd-86c8-0a945802917d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:32:32.774Z","request_id":"req_011CgS7giCTUfA2fiuZKCBke"}
