s (the log shows `similar=0`). From the second publish on, there's an existing story to compare against. A float score goes into the item, `put_item` raises `TypeError: Float types are not supported`, and the handler turns that into a 500. Every publish after the first will fail the same way. The existing test only ever published into an empty table, so it never computed a score.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **Conversion before the write:** a new `to_dynamo()` function runs on the whole item just before it's stored. It converts every float, at any depth, to a `Decimal`, and rejects NaN or infinity with a 400. Because it covers the whole item rather than just the score, a float added to any field later gets converted too.\n- **New tests:** one seeds the table with a story and then publishes a related one, one sends five publishes in a row through the handler, and one tests the converter on its own.\n\nThe new tests fail on the original code with the same error as production. With the fix, all 4 tests pass.\n\n**Colleague's note:** most of it applied. It's the same bug: a computed similarity score hidden by the empty table on the first write. I followed its advice to convert at the write boundary and to test against a table that already has a record. I didn't follow two parts:\n- **Integer percentages:** that would change the stored score scale from 0–1 to 0–100; `Decimal` keeps it as is.\n- **Publishing twice against a real table:** the ground rules rule that out. The repo's fake table checks values the way boto3 does and reproduces the production error.\n\nI also noticed that `publish()` reads existing stories with a single `scan()` and doesn't follow `LastEvaluatedKey` (the marker DynamoDB returns when there are more results). Once the table passes 1 MB, related stories will be picked from only part of the library. It's unrelated to the 500, so I noted it in `ANSWER.md` and left it alone.\n\nI haven't committed anything.","ttft_ms":4363,"type":"result","duration_ms":56103,"uuid"